<p>If your water damage claim was denied, the denial is not necessarily final. Most Texas water damage denials rest on a few predictable reasons: the damage was "gradual" instead of sudden, the water was "flood," the loss was caused by wear or poor maintenance, mold limits apply, or the claim was reported late. Each one can be challenged if the facts and the policy language support you.</p>
<p>This guide explains why insurers deny water claims, what Texas law requires of them, and the practical steps Houston-area homeowners can take to get a claim reopened, reappraised or escalated.</p>

<h2>Why Water Damage Claims Get Denied</h2>
<p>Water is one of the most common homeowners claims, and also one of the most disputed. The reason is simple: the policy covers some water and excludes a lot of it. Understanding which bucket your loss landed in is the first step to fighting a denial.</p>

<h3>Sudden vs. gradual damage</h3>
<p>The Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) says most homeowners and renters policies cover sudden and accidental water damage, such as a burst pipe, an overflowing toilet or a broken washer hose. Policies usually do not cover damage from gradual leaks or seepage.</p>
<p>That distinction drives a huge share of denials. An adjuster who sees rotted subflooring, staining in layers or long-term mold may conclude the leak has been going on for months. If the leak was actually hidden inside a wall and failed suddenly, you need evidence that says so: a plumber's written opinion on the failure point, photos of the broken fitting and a timeline of when you first noticed water.</p>

<h3>The flood exclusion</h3>
<p>Standard homeowners policies exclude flood. In practice, "flood" generally means surface water from outside, such as a rising bayou, street water pushing under doors or storm surge. Flood coverage comes from a separate policy, usually through the National Flood Insurance Program or a private flood insurer.</p>
<p>Insurers sometimes stretch the flood label. Water that came through a storm-damaged roof, or water from a supply line that failed during a storm, is not automatically flood. Ask exactly how the water entered the home and push back if the facts do not match the exclusion.</p>

<h3>Mold limits</h3>
<p>According to TDI, if mold grows on an item damaged by a covered event, that mold damage is covered. Mold from a gradual leak or a flood is not. TDI also notes that most home policies do not include mold cleanup and testing after the damaged item is removed, though some companies sell added coverage. In Houston's humidity, mold can start fast, so a slow response on your end can turn a covered loss into an excluded one.</p>

<h3>Wear, tear and maintenance exclusions</h3>
<p>Policies exclude deterioration, wear and tear, and damage from failing to maintain the home. Old caulk around a tub, a corroded water heater or a cracked shower pan may be called a maintenance issue. Even then, look closely: some policies exclude the failed part itself but still cover the resulting water damage to floors, drywall and cabinets.</p>

<h3>Late reporting</h3>
<p>TDI warns that you usually must report hidden water damage within days of first seeing it. Policies also require "prompt" notice and reasonable steps to prevent further damage. If you waited weeks, the insurer may argue the delay made the damage worse or made it impossible to investigate. Report as soon as you discover water, even if you are unsure it is covered.</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Denial reason</th><th>What the insurer is saying</th><th>Evidence that helps you</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Gradual leak</td><td>The damage happened slowly over time</td><td>Plumber's report on a sudden failure, photos of the broken part, dated timeline</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood</td><td>Surface water from outside caused the loss</td><td>Proof of the actual water source, roof or plumbing damage, no exterior water line</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mold exclusion or cap</td><td>Mold is limited or not covered</td><td>Proof the mold followed a covered water event and that you acted quickly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wear and tear or maintenance</td><td>The home was not maintained</td><td>Maintenance records, age of fixtures, policy language on resulting damage</td></tr>
