<h2>Get the Denial and Your Policy in Writing</h2>
<p>Texas law requires an insurer that rejects a claim to tell you in writing and state the reasons. If you only got a phone call, ask for the written decision. Then request a complete certified copy of your policy, including the declarations page and every endorsement, for the policy period when the loss happened.</p>
<p>With both documents in hand, compare the exact words of the exclusion the insurer cited against what happened. Ask for these items in a short, dated letter or email:</p>
<ul>
<li>The written denial with the specific policy provisions relied on</li>
<li>A certified copy of the full policy and all endorsements</li>
<li>The adjuster's inspection report, photos and any engineer or expert report</li>
<li>The estimate the insurer prepared, if any</li>
<li>Your claim file notes, if the insurer will provide them</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep every request in writing and keep copies. A clean paper trail is your strongest asset later.</p>

<h2>Know Your Rights Under the Texas Prompt Payment Act</h2>
<p>The Texas Prompt Payment of Claims Act, in Chapter 542 of the Texas Insurance Code, sets deadlines insurers must follow. According to TDI and the statute:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Acknowledge:</strong> within 15 days after you file a claim, the insurer must acknowledge it, begin its investigation and request the information it needs.</li>
<li><strong>Decide:</strong> it must accept or reject the claim in writing no later than 15 business days after it receives all the information it requested.</li>
<li><strong>Extend:</strong> if it needs more time, it must tell you why within that window and then decide within 45 more days.</li>
<li><strong>Pay:</strong> once it agrees to pay all or part of the claim, payment is generally due within 5 business days.</li>
</ul>
<p>TDI notes it can extend these deadlines after a declared disaster. If an insurer misses them, you may be able to recover statutory interest and attorney's fees. Weather-related claims, such as hail or hurricane damage, follow additional rules in Chapter 542A, including a written pre-suit notice requirement. Confirm how these rules apply to your claim with a Texas insurance attorney before relying on them.</p>

<h2>How to Get a Denied Claim Reopened</h2>
<p>Many denials are reversed because the homeowner supplies what the adjuster did not have. Start here before escalating.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Write a reconsideration letter.</strong> Quote the exclusion, explain why it does not fit the facts and attach your evidence. Ask for a written response.</li>
<li><strong>Get an independent cause-of-loss opinion.</strong> A licensed plumber, roofer or engineer who can say the failure was sudden carries real weight.</li>
<li><strong>Request a re-inspection.</strong> Ask for a different adjuster if the first inspection was rushed or missed areas.</li>
<li><strong>Document moisture.</strong> Moisture readings, thermal images and dated photos show the extent and age of the damage.</li>
<li><strong>Consider a licensed public adjuster.</strong> They represent you, not the insurer, and work on a fee. Our comparison of <a href="/blog/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster/">public adjusters vs. insurance adjusters</a> explains when one makes sense.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the insurer agreed to pay but undervalued the loss, check how it applied depreciation. Our <a href="/tools/insurance-claim-depreciation-calculator/">insurance claim depreciation calculator</a> shows how actual cash value and replacement cost differ.</p>

<h2>Appraisal: When the Fight Is About the Amount</h2>
<p>Most Texas homeowners policies include an appraisal clause. Either side can usually invoke it in writing when you agree the loss is covered but disagree on how much it is worth. Each side picks an appraiser, the two appraisers choose an umpire, and an agreement by any two of the three typically sets the amount of loss.</p>
<p>Appraisal is generally faster and cheaper than a lawsuit, but it has limits. It usually decides the amount of loss, not whether the policy covers the loss in the first place. If your claim was denied outright as gradual or flood, appraisal alone may not fix it. Read your policy's appraisal wording carefully, because the procedure and cost split vary by form.</p>

<h2>File a Complaint With the Texas Department of Insurance</h2>
<p>If talking to the insurer does not work, you can file a complaint with TDI online through its <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/complaint-home.html">consumer complaint page</a> or by calling the TDI Help Line at 800-252-3439. TDI will contact the company and ask for a response; TDI says home insurers have 25 days to respond.</p>
<p>A complaint is free and often prompts a closer review of the file. It has limits, though. TDI generally cannot settle disputes where you and the insurer disagree about the facts, and it cannot act as your lawyer. Use it alongside your own evidence, not instead of it.</p>
<p>Watch the clock while you work. Texas law generally does not allow a policy to shorten the time to sue below two years, but many policies set limits close to that, so check your policy's "suit against us" clause early. This is general information, not legal advice; for a large or complex denial, talk to a licensed Texas insurance attorney.</p>

<h2>Document Everything From Day One</h2>
<p>Strong documentation wins water claims. TDI recommends photographing and videotaping the damage and keeping damaged items until the adjuster sees them. It also advises making only temporary repairs and saving receipts, and not making permanent repairs until the insurer has seen the damage.</p>
<ul>
<li>Shoot wide and close-up photos and video before cleanup, including the water source.</li>
<li>Keep the failed part: the burst supply line, cracked fitting or failed valve.</li>
<li>Save every receipt for drying, fans, dehumidifiers, hotel stays and temporary repairs.</li>
<li>Log every call: date, name, what was said and what was promised.</li>
<li>Scan everything into one folder so you can send a complete package quickly.</li>
</ul>
<p>[[product:document-scanner]]</p>
<p>A pin-type moisture meter lets you record readings in the same spots over several days, which helps show whether walls are drying or whether there is a hidden source still leaking.</p>

<h2>Prevent the Next Water Claim</h2>
<p>The best denied claim is the one you never file. A few inexpensive habits and devices catch leaks before they become months-long "gradual" damage that insurers exclude.</p>
<ul>
<li>Put Wi-Fi leak sensors under sinks, behind toilets, under the water heater and beside the washer.</li>
<li>Replace rubber washer hoses with braided steel and check supply lines every year.</li>
<li>Re-caulk tubs, showers and sinks when caulk cracks or pulls away.</li>
<li>Know where your main shutoff is and test it.</li>
<li>Consider an automatic shutoff valve; some insurers offer discounts for them, so ask your agent.</li>
</ul>
<p>[[product:smart-water-shutoff]]</p>
<p>Our <a href="/blog/houston-home-maintenance-checklist/">Houston home maintenance checklist</a> covers the seasonal checks that keep plumbing and roofing problems from turning into claims.</p>

<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>A water damage claim denied is a starting point, not the end. Get the denial and full policy in writing, match the exclusion against the real facts, and answer it with expert opinions, photos and moisture data. Hold the insurer to Texas Prompt Payment deadlines, use appraisal when the dispute is about money, and file a TDI complaint or call a professional when the insurer will not budge.</p>
